fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1095", description: some(value: "WI-945's frame-read predicate counts THREE channels and there are FIVE: an op-scoped forward and an eta each load clean and die at eval.\n\nkernel-language.md:1344 states, as canonical spec, that \"'Reads the slot' counts three ways, and all three: the body DEFERS to it; it INHERITS ...; or it FORWARDS ...\". That completeness claim is FALSE. Two more channels reach the same frame, both are reachable, and both produce the exact outcome that same paragraph forbids -- 'deferring the failure to evaluation would be a clean load that crashes at run time'. Found by /code-review on the WI-939 branch, with measured repros and both-sides controls; NOT fixed there (typing surgery plus a spec correction, past that ticket's size).\n\nFOUR AND FIVE, each measured on a probe added to wi945_unconstrained_sort_element_test.rs, run, then reverted:\n\n(4) AN OP-SCOPED FORWARD. typing.rs:16108-16121 -- the 'Step::Forwards' arm destructures 'ConcreteApplyWithin { fn_target_sym, dispatch_dict, .. }' and reads ONLY 'dispatch_dict'. The same variant carries 'op_dicts', filled by 'build_op_scoped_dicts' through 'build_dep_projection' against 'env.enclosing_frame_chain()', which can project a slot as 'var_ref(__req_<sort-half-slot>)' -- a read of THIS frame, exactly the shape the third arm was added for. MEASURED: a 'HolderVS' with 'sort V = ? / sort F = ? / requires VectorSpace[V, F] / requires Doubler[V]' whose 'twice' calls a cross-sort 'Inner.innerOp[W](a: W) -> W requires Doubler[W]', with a real 'provides Doubler[V = Vec3]' in scope, LOADS CLEAN and dies Internal(\\\"DeferToRequirement: requirement param __req_doubler not bound in caller frame (running Inner.innerOp; frame binds [])\\\"). CONTROL: the identical program with 'requires VectorSpace[V = V, F = Float]' loads clean and answers Vec3(1,2,3) -- so the crash is attributable to the unconstrained 'F' and to nothing else in the fixture.\n\n(5) AN ETA. 'CallClass::EtaOpRef' is not matched at all: it falls through to '_ => Step::Nothing'. 'attach_eta_dispatch_dict' (typing.rs:6721/6735/6750) mints 'dict = Some(var_ref(__req_self))' for a same-sort eta and a 'build_concrete_dispatch_dict' result -- which may itself contain caller-frame forwards -- for a cross-sort one, and 'op_dicts' rides the same classification. MEASURED: the same 'HolderVS' with 'operation helper(a: V) -> V = VectorSpace.vec_add(a, a)' and 'operation twice(a: V) -> V = Ap.ap(helper, a)' LOADS CLEAN and dies Internal(\\\"var_ref(__req_self) unbound in requirement position (running HolderVS.twice; frame binds [])\\\"). CONTROL: with 'requires VectorSpace[V = V, F = Float]' the same program answers Vec3(2,4,6).\n\nTHE SECOND HALF OF (5) IS THE TRAP, and wiring the classification alone does not close it. 'sort_names' (typing.rs:16093) is 'chain.names(kb)[..sort_len]' -- the '__req_<spec>' names ONLY. '__req_self' is not among them, so feeding 'EtaOpRef.dict' to 'dict_forwards_frame_slot' would still find nothing. The frame-slot SET needs '__req_self' too. Two edits, one channel; a fix that makes only the first will look correct and change no verdict.\n\nTHESE ARE UNSTATED GAPS, NOT WITHHELD REFUSALS. The doc comment at typing.rs:16043-16049 enumerates 'WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FOLLOW' -- a same-sort 'PinNow', and a cross-sort callee's own body -- and argues each is a refusal withheld rather than a wrong value. NEITHER exclusion covers these two: an op-scoped forward and an eta are reads of THIS frame, which is the predicate's own subject. So the bound the comment states is not the bound the code has.\n\nFAMILY / WHY THE COUNT KEEPS BEING WRONG: WI-945 itself shipped with TWO channels and gained the third from /code-review after a measured clean-load/eval-crash ('a_forwarded_slot_inside_a_built_dictionary_is_refused_too' pins that one). This is the same defect a second time, and the pattern -- 'many producers, one rule; find them all' -- says the fix must enumerate the CallClass variants that can carry a dictionary rather than add two more arms and stop. WI-822 LEG 2 measured the same supply-vs-read split on the op half.\n\nACCEPTANCE:\n - both shapes REFUSED AT LOAD, each naming the requirement and the unconstrained element, driven by a test that reproduces the eval crash when the fix is backed out -- not a load-clean assertion;\n - BOTH controls kept and stated at the site: the 'F = Float' twin of each fixture must load clean AND answer its value, so the refusal is attributable to the unpinned element;\n - the '__req_self' half of (5) driven by its own row, so a fix that wires the classification and not the frame-slot set FAILS;\n - the CallClass variants that can carry a dictionary enumerated at the predicate, so a sixth channel is a compile error or a loud fallthrough rather than a silent '_ => Nothing';\n - kernel-language.md:1344 corrected -- the sentence says 'three ways, and all three' and must state the real count, or state the enumeration and its bound;\n - typing.rs:16043-16049's 'WHAT IT DELIBERATELY DOES NOT FOLLOW' updated so the stated bound matches the code;\n - cargo-test green, full workspace via scripts/test.sh (core types => FULL workspace).\n\nSOURCE: /code-review high on the WI-939 branch (commit deb903ea), findings 1-3. The reviewer's own coverage notes recorded as verified-clean: Term::subterms() does reach nested var_refs in named args; for_each_child covers match arms / let bodies / lambdas; 'sources' stays parallel to 'errors'; one global type_check_sorts call and two build_concrete_dispatch_dict callers, both wired."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none), ToolPasses(tool: "scaland-sbt-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-15T04:12:41Z"))
